  <h2 id="what-are-dividends">What Are Dividends?</h2>
  <p>A dividend is a cash payment a company makes to its shareholders, usually every quarter.</p>
  <h3>Key Metrics</h3>
  <div class="table-wrapper"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>What It Tells You</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Dividend Yield</strong></td><td>Annual dividend as % of stock price</td><td>Annual Dividend &divide; Share Price</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Payout Ratio</strong></td><td>% of earnings paid as dividends</td><td>Dividends &divide; Net Income</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DPS Growth</strong></td><td>YoY dividend growth</td><td>(DPS1 &minus; DPS0) &divide; DPS0</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ex-Dividend Date</strong></td><td>Must own stock before this date</td><td>Set by exchange</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Payout Frequency</strong></td><td>How often dividends are paid</td><td>Quarterly / Monthly / Annual</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <h2>How to Start</h2>
  <div class="timeline"><div class="timeline-item"><div class="timeline-marker">1</div><div class="timeline-body"><h3>Open a brokerage account</h3><p>Choose a low-cost broker (Fidelity, Schwab, Vanguard) with commission-free trades and DRIP support.</p></div></div><div class="timeline-item"><div class="timeline-marker">2</div><div class="timeline-body"><h3>Decide on a strategy</h3><p>High yield or dividend growth? Read our <a href="/strategy">Strategy page</a>.</p></div></div><div class="timeline-item"><div class="timeline-marker">3</div><div class="timeline-body"><h3>Research dividend stocks</h3><p>Yield &ge; 2%, payout ratio &lt; 60%, 5+ years growth. Start with our <a href="/stocks">stock list</a>.</p></div></div><div class="timeline-item"><div class="timeline-marker">4</div><div class="timeline-body"><h3>Enable DRIP and DCA</h3><p>Turn on automatic dividend reinvestment. Invest a fixed amount monthly.</p></div></div><div class="timeline-item"><div class="timeline-marker">5</div><div class="timeline-body"><h3>Monitor and rebalance</h3><p>Review your portfolio annually. Cut declining stocks. Reinvest into new opportunities.</p></div></div></div>
  <h2>Types of Dividend Stocks</h2>
  <div class="card-grid"><div class="card"><div class="card-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polygon points="12 2 15.09 8.26 22 9.27 17 14.14 18.18 21.02 12 17.77 5.82 21.02 7 14.14 2 9.27 8.91 8.26 12 2"/></svg></div><h3>Dividend Aristocrats</h3><p>S&P 500 companies that increased dividends for <strong>25+ consecutive years</strong>.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="card-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="23 6 13.5 15.5 8.5 10.5 1 18"/><polyline points="17 6 23 6 23 12"/></svg></div><h3>Dividend Growth</h3><p>Companies growing dividends <strong>10%+ annually</strong>. Lower current yield, rapid compounding.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="card-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="22 7 13.5 15.5 8.5 10.5 2 17"/><polyline points="16 7 22 7 22 13"/></svg></div><h3>High Yield</h3><p>Yields of <strong>4-8%+</strong> &mdash; REITs, MLPs, BDCs. Watch for dividend sustainability.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="card-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><rect x="2" y="7" width="20" height="14" rx="2"/><path d="M16 21V5a2 2 0 0 0-2-2h-4a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16"/></svg></div><h3>Monthly Payers</h3><p>Stocks paying <strong>every month</strong>. Great for cash-flow investors.</p></div></div>
  <h2>Risks</h2>
  <div class="tip-box"><strong>Warning:</strong> A 10% yield might signal a company in distress. Always check payout ratio before chasing yield.</div>
  <ul><li><strong>Dividend cuts</strong> &mdash; Companies can reduce or eliminate dividends during downturns.</li><li><strong>Interest rate sensitivity</strong> &mdash; Rising bond yields compete with dividend stocks.</li><li><strong>Sector concentration</strong> &mdash; Dividend payers cluster in utilities, financials, energy.</li><li><strong>Tax drag</strong> &mdash; Taxable accounts create annual tax liability even if you reinvest.</li></ul>
  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <details class="faq-item" open><summary>Are dividends free money?</summary><div class="faq-answer">No. When a company pays a $1 dividend, the stock price typically drops by ~$1 on the ex-dividend date.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>How much to invest to live off dividends?</summary><div class="faq-answer">At 4% yield, you need ~$1M for $40K/year. Use our <a href="/calculator">calculator</a> to run your numbers.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>Qualified vs non-qualified?</summary><div class="faq-answer"><strong>Qualified</strong> (US stocks held &gt;60 days): taxed at 0-20%. <strong>Non-qualified</strong> (REITs, foreign): taxed as ordinary income.</div></details>

